---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout title="Browser Demo & Offline — Varve Docs" description="Use the Varve browser demo, install it as an app, work offline, and understand exactly what is saved and where.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Browser Demo
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Browser Demo &amp; Offline</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        The browser demo at <a href={sitePath('/try/')}>varve.studio/try/</a> is the real editor
        running on the WASM engine, entirely on your device. Nothing is uploaded, no account is
        required, and the same document format opens in the desktop app. This guide covers what is
        actually saved, how offline operation works, and what the demo deliberately leaves out.
        On a Chromebook, compare this route with the installed app and the Linux application in{' '}
        <a href={sitePath('/docs/chromebook')}>Varve on Chromebook</a>.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>What the demo is</h2>
        <p>
          The demo is a bounded browser build, not a hosted replacement for the desktop app. It
          includes the Design, Draw, and Photo workspaces with the shared scene model, tools,
          undo/redo, layers, and Inspector. Print production (PDF, CMYK, bleed, colour-managed
          output), on-device inference (background removal, upscaling, visual search), and the
          Motion, Codegen, Logo, and Email workspaces remain desktop-only, and the interface says so
          where those features would appear.
        </p>
        <p>
          The engine loads as WebAssembly and renders with Canvas2D in a browser tab. On this host
          the page is not cross-origin isolated, so multi-threaded WASM is unavailable and large
          documents render more slowly than in the desktop application.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Constrained devices and rendering</h2>
        <p>
          The browser build measures the cadence of the actual animation frames
          and keeps a small amount of CPU and memory headroom. During an active
          drag or zoom it may use a lower preview backing scale, then promotes
          the settled frame back to the full device-pixel resolution. Text,
          selection handles, hit testing, exports, and saved originals keep
          their document coordinates and fidelity; the preview is never a
          resampling of your file.
        </p>
        <p>
          Thumbnails, raster pyramids, and semantic indexing are background
          work. They are bounded, cancellable, and paused when the page is
          hidden or frozen so pointer and keyboard input keeps priority. A
          browser may still discard a tab under memory pressure, so save
          important work to a real file and keep the recovery copy as a safety
          net.
        </p>
        <p>
          WebGPU is attempted only after an adapter and device can be created;
          a failed or lost device returns to the Canvas2D path. The browser
          build does not claim access to a Chromebook NPU. The optional
          on-device model catalog is currently a desktop feature; core browser
          editing never waits for a model download.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Opening the demo</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Open <a href={sitePath('/try/')}>varve.studio/try/</a> in a current Chrome, Edge, or other Chromium-based browser. Firefox and Safari work with the download save fallback described below.</li>
          <li>A sample document opens directly in the editor.</li>
          <li>Edit, draw, or import your own PNG/JPEG/SVG artwork. Everything stays in this browser.</li>
          <li>When you want to keep a file, use <strong>File → Save As</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) so you choose where it goes.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Saving, recovery, and what "Saved" means</h2>
        <p>
          The demo follows your browser's file capabilities. In Chromium browsers it can keep
          writing to a file you chose (File System Access API), re-asking for permission when the
          browser requires it. In Firefox and Safari, Save produces a fresh snapshot download each
          time and the browser-local copy is updated as well, because a download is not a
          persistent location.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Saved means the write to the destination actually finished.</strong> The status
          bar reports <em>Saving…</em>, <em>Saved</em>, <em>Modified</em>, <em>Not saved</em>, or{' '}
          <em>Save failed</em> with the reason in its tooltip. A cancelled file picker changes
          nothing and does not mark the document saved. While edits have not reached a destination,
          the demo writes <strong>recovery copies</strong> into this browser's storage; they are
          offered after a crash or a discarded tab and are removed when the document is saved, when
          you discard it intentionally, or after seven days.
        </p>
        <p>
          The demo does not auto-save to disk. Set your own save rhythm, or use the desktop app's
          file workflow for continuous work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Installing as an app</h2>
        <p>
          In Chrome or Edge on desktop and Chromebooks, the demo can be installed like an app:
          click the install icon in the address bar (or <strong>⋮ → Cast, save, and share →
          Install page as app</strong>). It then opens in its own window with a launcher icon.
          Installed and tab sessions share the same browser storage, so documents are the same in
          both.
        </p>
        <p>
          If install is unavailable (policy, an unsupported browser, or a private window), keep
          using the demo in a normal tab — every feature behaves identically. Uninstalling the app
          with "delete data" removes documents and recovery copies; export what you need first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Offline</h2>
        <p>
          Offline operation is real but staged. After one complete online visit, the browser
          service worker has saved the application shell, its scripts, styles, fonts, and the WASM
          engine, and <strong>the demo can then open and edit offline</strong>. The demo banner
          tells you when you are running from the saved copy and that edits stay on this device.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>First-ever visit offline:</strong> no application code has ever run in this browser, so nothing can display an editor. The page explains that the demo is not available offline yet and asks you to reconnect.</li>
          <li><strong>Interrupted setup:</strong> if the browser installed the worker but the offline copy is incomplete, a plain page says the copy is not finished and that your saved documents were not touched.</li>
          <li><strong>Private/incognito windows</strong> discard everything when they close and cannot install apps.</li>
          <li><strong>Updates catch up later:</strong> if the demo updates itself, the new version downloads in the background and asks before reloading.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Storage: what is kept, and how to clean it up</h2>
        <p>
          Open <strong>File → Settings… → Storage &amp; Offline</strong> to see the browser's
          estimate of storage used, whether the browser has granted persistent storage, how many
          recovery copies exist, and how many offline app files are cached.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Ask the browser to keep it</strong> requests persistent storage. Browsers may decide based on how often you use the site; a declined request deletes nothing.</li>
          <li><strong>Clear offline app copies</strong> removes only the cached copy of the application itself so the next visit downloads a fresh build. Documents and recovery copies are untouched; offline support returns after one complete online visit.</li>
          <li><strong>"Delete browsing data"</strong> in browser settings (or uninstalling the app with data removal) deletes documents and recovery copies too. Export anything you need first.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Browser storage estimates are approximate and shared with everything else this site
          stores. Plan for roughly twice a document's size while a save is in progress. If the
          device runs low on space, the browser may evict best-effort site data — another reason to
          save important work to a real file or to the desktop app.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Files on a Chromebook</h2>
        <p>
          Saving uses the ChromeOS Files app. When you choose a location, Chrome
          asks for access and can remember it; if permission expires, the next
          save asks again and your edits stay put until the write succeeds.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>My Files and removable media</strong> work like any other folder. If an SD card or USB drive is removed while a document is open, the next save reports the destination is unavailable — reconnect it and save again, or use Save As.</li>
          <li><strong>Google Drive files</strong> can be opened and saved through the Files app, but Drive sync is handled by ChromeOS, not Varve. If a newer copy was synced from another device, Varve refuses to overwrite it and points you at Save As, so both versions survive.</li>
          <li><strong>Offline Drive copies</strong> are your Files app's "available offline" setting. Varve does not manage which Drive files stay offline.</li>
          <li><strong>Read-only or shared files</strong> cannot be overwritten; the save reports a permission failure and Save As creates your own copy.</li>
          <li><strong>Deleting browsing data or uninstalling the app with data removal</strong> deletes documents and recovery copies stored in the browser. Files saved through the Files app live outside browser storage and are not removed by that.</li>
          <li><strong>Private/incognito windows</strong> discard everything at the end of the session; save to a file if the work matters.</li>
          <li><strong>ChromeOS Linux (the Linux development environment)</strong> keeps its own files; storage there is separate from the browser demo.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Updates</h2>
        <p>
          A new build is never forced onto an open document. When an update is ready the demo shows
          a notice with <strong>Update and reload</strong> and <strong>Dismiss</strong>; the reload
          happens only when you choose it. If an old page references build files that a deploy has
          replaced, the same notice recovers the session with a reload — your documents are in
          browser storage, not in those files.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Moving work between browser and desktop</h2>
        <p>
          Documents use the same <code>.varve</code> format in both. Save from the demo, then open
          the file in the desktop app (or vice versa). The demo exports PNG, JPEG, WebP, and SVG;
          PDF and print production require the desktop app. Optional on-device models, local font
          installation, and native file paths are desktop features.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Touch, pen, and keyboard on a tablet</h2>
        <p>
          The browser demo adapts to tablet and split-screen widths instead of assuming a desktop
          window. At narrow widths the Layers, Resources, and Inspector panels become on-screen
          drawers, the tool palette and status bar stay reachable, and controls grow to
          comfortable touch targets.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One finger</strong> draws or selects with the active tool by default. Settings &gt; Drawing input can switch one-finger touch to viewport navigation.</li>
          <li><strong>Two fingers</strong> cancel only a provisional first-pointer action, then pan and pinch-zoom the canvas around the gesture; page zoom is not affected. Lift both fingers and start a new contact before drawing again.</li>
          <li><strong>Long-press</strong> a stack of overlapping layers for the deep-selection menu — the touch equivalent of right-click.</li>
          <li><strong>Pen</strong> input is treated as a precision pointer: pressure, tilt, and twist are used when the browser and device report useful values, and strokes fall back to a stable constant response when they do not. Eraser-tip input is preserved where the platform exposes it, but real pressure, palm rejection, hover, and USI button behavior are not certified by browser emulation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          A detachable keyboard is optional. The app adapts to the actual viewport and pointer
          capabilities, never to assumptions about whether a keyboard is attached. Where the
          browser reports the on-screen keyboard geometry, dialogs, toasts, and the panel drawer
          buttons move above it so the field you are editing and its commit controls stay visible.
          The full touch, pen, keyboard, trackpad, and accessibility reference is{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/touch-and-pen')}>Touch, pen, keyboard, and trackpad</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Portrait and landscape are treated as a width change, not two different apps. In
          portrait the inspector and library open as bottom sheets; in landscape they stay at the
          side. Rotating with a panel open keeps it open and does not touch your document,
          selection, or scroll position. On a Chromebook tablet, a swipe from the left edge —
          the system's back gesture — closes the open menu or dialog instead of leaving the page.
        </p>
        <p>
          ChromeOS owns some keyboard combinations. Varve switches workspaces with{' '}
          <code>Ctrl+Shift+1…7</code> (not the browser's <code>Ctrl+1…8</code> tab switches).
          Combinations such as <code>Ctrl+T</code>, <code>Ctrl+W</code>,
          <code>Ctrl+Shift+I</code>, and <code>Ctrl+P</code> belong to the browser, so use the
          menu, tab strip, or command palette for those actions in the browser demo.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>"Varve is not available offline yet"</strong> — the browser has no complete offline copy. Reconnect, load the demo once, and let the page settle for a few seconds.</li>
          <li><strong>The demo asks for file access again</strong> — browsers do not always remember file permissions between sessions. Choose the same file again, or use <strong>Save As</strong>.</li>
          <li><strong>Save failed with a permission or quota message</strong> — the document stays modified and its recovery copy remains. Free up space or grant access, then save again; nothing was overwritten by the failed attempt.</li>
          <li><strong>An update notice will not go away after updating</strong> — close every demo tab and window (including the installed app) and reopen; one old client can keep an update "waiting".</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }
  .docs-page h1 { font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }

  .docs-section ul {
    list-style: none;
  }
  .docs-section ol {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }
  .docs-section ol li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
  .docs-section code {
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    padding: 0.125rem 0.375rem;
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-control-compact);
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }
  .docs-section kbd {
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-default);
    border-radius: var(--website-radius-control-compact);
    padding: 0.125rem 0.375rem;
    font-family: monospace;
    font-size: var(--type-interface-caption-size);
  }
</style>
